You ask an AI tool for an HTTP client wrapper for an internal service call. It hands back:

```python
def call_service(url, payload, timeout=30):
    return requests.post(url, json=payload, timeout=timeout)
```

Thirty seconds is a completely defensible default timeout for a general-purpose HTTP helper — it shows up constantly in tutorials and library defaults. It is also nineteen seconds longer than the 11-second hard limit your API gateway enforces before it kills the request and returns a 504 anyway, which means every legitimately slow downstream call now fails twice as expensively: the gateway kills it at 11 seconds, and nothing in this function's timeout ever gets a chance to matter, while a caller waiting on this function has no way to know the real ceiling was 11 seconds all along.

